# Citation File Format v1.2.0
# https://citation-file-format.github.io/
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  AI Security & Jailbreak Defence: an educational course teaching the
  model-and-prompt layer of LLM security through intentionally
  vulnerable models
message: >-
  If you use this course (in teaching, in your own AI-security training
  materials, or as a primary source in a research paper), please cite it
  using the metadata below.
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Kereopa-Yorke
    given-names: Benjamin
repository-code: "https://github.com/Benjamin-KY/AISecurityModel"
url: "https://github.com/Benjamin-KY/AISecurityModel"
abstract: >-
  An 18-notebook educational course on AI security and jailbreak defence,
  built around a deliberately vulnerable LoRA-fine-tuned Qwen2.5-3B
  adapter (Zen0/Vulnerable-Edu-Qwen3B). The course is organised into
  three tracks: a **foundational track** (notebooks 1-6) covering the
  attack/defence basics at the model-and-prompt layer, an **advanced
  track** (notebooks 7-15) covering automated red-teaming, monitoring,
  multi-agent security, industry-specific compliance, fine-tuning
  robustness, multi-modal security, supply-chain security, and incident
  response and forensics, and a **2026 architectural capstone track**
  (notebooks 16-18) covering agent / MCP tool misuse, RAG-layer prompt
  injection, and the harness paradigm — explicitly handing off to the
  sibling `harmless-harnesses` course for the full architectural-layer
  curriculum. The course follows a vulnerable-then-educate pattern:
  every notebook first demonstrates a working attack against the
  vulnerable model, then teaches the defence pattern that mitigates it.
  All compliance prose references Australian statutes (Privacy Act 1988
  including the Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2024,
  APRA CPS 234, ACSC Essential Eight, AI Ethics Principles, the
  Voluntary AI Safety Standard) by name. The course ships with a working
  Gradio Space comparing vulnerable and defended modes, an educator
  guide with five course formats (2 h workshop → 5-day intensive),
  a strategic `POSITIONING.md` mapping this repository's role in the
  three-repository Kereopa-Yorke constellation, an operational
  `READING_ORDER.md` with six audience-specific paths, fine-tuning
  scripts to regenerate the vulnerable adapter, and an
  OWASP-LLM-Top-10-mapped vulnerability taxonomy. The course code is
  Apache-2.0 and the course content is CC BY-SA 4.0.
keywords:
  - AI security
  - LLM safety
  - jailbreak defence
  - prompt injection
  - red-teaming
  - vulnerable-by-design model
  - LoRA fine-tuning
  - OWASP LLM Top 10
  - Australian compliance
  - APRA CPS 234
  - Privacy Act 1988
  - ACSC Essential Eight
  - AI ethics
  - security education
license:
  - Apache-2.0
  - CC-BY-SA-4.0
version: 2.4.0
date-released: 2026-06-15
references:
  - type: software
    title: "harmless-harnesses"
    authors:
      - family-names: Kereopa-Yorke
        given-names: Benjamin
    repository-code: "https://github.com/Benjamin-KY/Harmless-Harnesses"
    abstract: >-
      Companion course teaching the structural-harness layer of LLM
      governance (policy router, source authority, prompt composer,
      output-contract enforcement, audit log + escalation FSM).
      Where this course teaches the model and prompt layer, harmless-
      harnesses teaches the scaffolding around the model.
